,title,link,text,language,family
0,Yuman music - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yuman_music,"Yuman music is the music of Yumans, a group of Native American tribes from what is now Southern California and Baja California. They include Paipai, Havasupai, Yavapai, Walapai, Mohave, Quechan, Maricopa, Tipai-Ipai, Cocopa, and Kiliwa people. [1] Folk songs in Yuma culture are said to be given to a person while dreaming. Many individuals who are in emotional distress go to a secluded area for ...",Maricopa,Cochimí-Yuman
1,Maricopa people - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maricopa_people,"The Maricopa at the Salt River Pima-Maricopa Indian Community consist mostly of Xalychidom Piipaash members and are concentrated in Lehi. [3] The Maricopa at the Gila River Indian Community are concentrated in Maricopa Colony. The Maricopa are a River Yuman group, formerly living along the banks of the Colorado River.",Maricopa,Cochimí-Yuman
2,Yuman-Cochimi Languages - Sorosoro,https://www.sorosoro.org/en/yuman-cochimi-languages/,"Cochimi Cochimi: Extinct Comments on the classification of Yuman-Cochimi languages: The term ""Hokan"" describes a hypothetical set of language families that would include the Palaihnihan languages, but also Yuman-Cochimi languages and some isolated languages of the western United States (primarily from California and north-western Mexico).",Maricopa,Cochimí-Yuman
3,Yuman music - Wikiwand,https://www.wikiwand.com/en/articles/Yuman_music,"Yuman music is the music of Yumans, a group of Native American tribes from what is now Southern California and Baja California. They include Paipai, Havasupai, Yavapai, Walapai, Mohave, Quechan, Maricopa, Tipai-Ipai, Cocopa, and Kiliwa people. [1] Folk songs in Yuma culture are said to be given to a person while dreaming. Many individuals who are in emotional distress go to a secluded area for ...",Maricopa,Cochimí-Yuman
4,Yuman Family - Legends of America,https://www.legendsofamerica.com/yuman-family/,"The Yuman Family is an important linguistic group comprising various Native American tribes and bands that traditionally lived in the lower Colorado River and Gila River Valleys in western Arizona and southern California, in the United States, and in northern Baja California and northwestern Sonora, Mexico. They spoke related languages of the Hokan language stock.",Maricopa,Cochimí-Yuman
5,The Yuman Musical Style,https://www.jstor.org/stable/534896,The rhythmical devices mentioned are widely found in American Indian music and more or less in all primitive music. In the Yuman style the employment of such means results in considerable variety of forms.,Maricopa,Cochimí-Yuman
6,Yuman-Cochimi languages - YouTube,https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PL67dm6qliEMCBkczhKEX1sDgy0Nuepohi,"Share your videos with friends, family, and the world",Maricopa,Cochimí-Yuman
7,Songs Of The Yaqui - Drumhop,https://drumhop.com/music.php?page=103,"Songs Of The Yaqui Recorded and Edited by Frances Densmore Folk Music Of The United States Issued from the Collections of the Archive of American Folk Song L24 Two distinct cultures are represented in this series of songs. The Yuma and Cocopa belong to a group of tribes known as the Yuman, whose early home was the valley of the Colorado River.",Maricopa,Cochimí-Yuman
8,Traditional Songs of the Salt River Pima - Canyon Records,https://canyonrecords.bandcamp.com/album/traditional-songs-of-the-salt-river-pima,"A member of the Salt River Pima-Maricopa tribe, Earl Ray (known in Pima as Lo:dac) is a singer, linguist, and tribal activist and is currently the only one who reads and writes the Salt River Pima dialect. On this recording, Earl sings a collection of rare and historic songs evoking the rich mythology and cultural pageantry of the 'Akimel 'O'odham.",Maricopa,Cochimí-Yuman
9,Songs of The Colorado | ASU Events,https://asuevents.asu.edu/event/songs-colorado,"Songs of the Colorado is a documentary film that tells the story of the traditional songs of the Yuman-speaking people and how those songs connect them, through story, language and history. At least 10 Indigenous tribes along the Colorado River and across the border in Mexico speak the Yuman language, including the Cucapa, Havasupai, Quechan, Mojave, Maricopa, Kumeyaay, Ipai/Tipai and Yapavai ...",Maricopa,Cochimí-Yuman
